# 06_class_style_toggle
# ---------------------
# MEASURED 2026-08-18 on 755f6e19, clean tree, chromium 147.0.7727.137
# (evidence: ~/.hamnix-build/tok-livedom-rerun-20260818/livedom-clean.log).
# The whole diff is ONE line, and it is ONE attribute:
#
#     chromium:   2 E div [class=open data-state=open id=box style=display: block; color: red;]
#     hambrowse:  2 E div [class=open data-state=open id=box]
#
# THE style ATTRIBUTE IS ABSENT, NOT MISFORMATTED. The fixture sets
# `box.style.display = 'block'` and `box.style.color = 'red'`; the CSSOM
# property store takes the write and NOTHING mirrors it into the `style`
# content attribute, so el.attributes / getAttribute('style') never see it.
# This is the SAME two-writers-one-reader shape as D1, D2b, D3, D4, D6 and the
# handler-attribute gap — the ninth instance in this lane — and NOT what
# BASELINE's note claimed. That note said 06 "diverges on the style= attribute
# alone ... closing it also needs CSSOM serialisation to match chromium
# (declaration order and 'prop: value;' spacing, plus colour normalisation)".
# The serialisation half is a real second obstacle and it is still true, but it
# is DOWNSTREAM: there is no serialisation to argue about while the mirror does
# not exist at all. Anyone picking this up should expect to write the mirror
# first and only then discover whether the spacing matches.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT CLOSED HERE: mirroring the CSSOM store into the content
# attribute changes what `style=` reads back for every element on every page,
# and scripts/test_hambrowse_style_host.sh banks an expectation over that same
# store. That is a second gate to re-measure, not a line to add, and it was out
# of scope for the commit that made this gate's exit status honest.
#
# The consequence on a real site, stated plainly: any page that sets an inline
# style from script and then READS it back through getAttribute('style') — the
# ordinary "is this element already shown" check, and what a framework's
# reconciler diffs against — gets nothing back, forever.
